  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a randomized initialization combined with a gradient-based optimization to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. Unlike the previous approach relying on fixed cluster structures, this method starts with a random configuration that satisfies the integral constraint and then performs local optimization. The key innovation is leveraging randomized initialization to escape local minima, while using gradient-based steps to fine-tune the solution. This approach avoids getting stuck in suboptimal local optima from structured initial guesses. The bottleneck addressed is the poor exploration of the solution space due to fixed cluster positions, which can lead to suboptimal solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h(x) is represented as a step function with n_points samples over [0, 2]. Let h be a vector of length n_points where each entry h[i] \u2208 [0, 1], and dx = 2/n_points. The integral constraint is \u2211(h[i]) * dx = 1, which translates to \u2211(h[i]) = n_points / 2. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx, which is equivalent to the maximum of the correlation between h and (1 - h) scaled by dx. The problem is to find h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points that minimizes this objective while satisfying the constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent validation and initialization: If a parent solution is provided, use it as a base. Otherwise, generate a random initial guess by:\n   i. Setting h[i] = 1 for n_points / 2 random indices\n   ii. Normalizing the sum to n_points / 2\n   \nb. Optimization loop:\n   i. Define the objective function as the maximum correlation value\n   ii. Perform a constrained optimization using the L-BFGS-B solver\n   iii. Apply the following during optimization:\n      - Clip values to [0, 1]\n      - Normalize the sum to maintain \u2211(h) = n_points / 2 after each iteration\n      - Use the provided objective function for evaluation\n   \nc. Perturbation and restart:\n   i. After every 50 iterations, introduce small random perturbations (\u00b10.05) to the current solution\n   ii. Re-normalize the sum to maintain the constraint\n   iii. If the objective improves, retain the new solution; otherwise, revert to the previous state\n   \nd. Termination:\n   i. Run for 1000 iterations\n   ii. Track the best solution found during the process\n   iii. Stop early if no improvement is detected for 50 consecutive iterations\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Default configuration:\n   - n_points = 400 (same as previous approach)\n   - Initial random seed = 42\n   - Perturbation strength = 0.05\n   - Maximum iterations = 1000\n   - Perturbation frequency = 50 iterations\n   \nb. Variations:\n   1. Different initialization: Try using initial cluster positions instead of random for better coverage\n   2. Varying perturbation magnitude (0.025 to 0.1) to explore different regions of the solution space\n   3. Adjusting the number of points (n_points) to 600 or 800 for higher resolution if time allows\n   4. Using a different optimizer (e.g., SLSQP) for initial refinement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na. Time allocation:\n   - 90% of time (540s) to the optimization loop\n   - 10% (60s) to perturbation and restart phases\n   \nb. Early termination checks:\n   - Monitor for stagnation (no improvement for 50 iterations)\n   - If time runs out, return the best solution found so far\n   \nc. Robustness measures:\n   - Fallback to the initial solution if optimization fails\n   - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] through clipping and normalization\n   - Validate that \u2211(h) == n_points / 2 after every iteration\n   - If numerical instability occurs, reduce perturbation magnitude and restart\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na. Feasibility checks:\n   - Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1]\n   - Confirm \u2211(h) == n_points / 2\n   - Ensure the correlation computation matches the evaluator's method\n   \nb. Objective validation:\n   - Re-evaluate the best solution using the provided evaluator\n   - Compare with the previous best value to ensure improvement\n   \nc. Interface requirements:\n   - Return h_values as a list of floats\n   - Provide c5_bound as the computed maximum overlap\n   - Include n_points as the number of samples",
